      "where": "Preprocessing, p. 5: 'we used the samples between 100ms and 900ms' - a window is given, an aggregator is not",
      "assumed": "Arithmetic mean of every tracked coordinate, then cast the ray (mean-then-cast, not cast-then-mean)",
      "sensitivity": "Moderate. The two orders differ by second-order terms in the tremor amplitude the paper itself discusses (8-12 Hz); negligible for EFRC, larger for IFRC and FRC where 1 degree of direction error is about 3.5 cm on the screen at 2 m"
    },
    {
      "question": "Is the 100-900 ms window measured from the button press or from target onset?",
      "where": "Procedure, p. 5: participants 'click ... when they started to hold a gesture' and 'the target disappeared after one second' - both define a plausible zero",
      "assumed": "From the button press that starts the one-second hold",
      "sensitivity": "Moderate. Anchoring at target onset would place part of the window in the movement phase and inflate every offset"
    },
    {
      "question": "Over which grouping is the two-standard-deviation outlier bound computed, and is it one- or two-sided?",
      "where": "Preprocessing, p. 5: 'using two times the standard deviation as an upper bound', with no grouping",
      "assumed": "Per ray cast x environment x target, one-sided on the distance, following the predecessor paper's explicit rule",
      "sensitivity": "Moderate and unfalsifiable: global versus per-target filtering changes both the retained N and the mean offsets, and the paper reports neither the retained N nor the removal count"
    },
    {
      "question": "What transform does 'we aligned the heads of the participants' apply?",
      "where": "Preprocessing, p. 5",
      "assumed": "Translate every tracked position of a participant by (grand-mean head position - that participant's mean head position)",
      "sensitivity": "High for the fit: it defines the shared frame the universal polynomial lives in. A vertical-only shift, a full rigid alignment or a per-trial re-centring all yield different alpha distributions and therefore different coefficients"
    },
    {
      "question": "Is the leave-one-out cross-validation leave-one-participant-out or leave-one-trial-out?",
      "where": "Modeling, p. 6: 'we used leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV)'",
      "assumed": "Leave-one-participant-out, following the predecessor paper, which states it explicitly; exposed as --cv participant|trial",
      "sensitivity": "High. Leave-one-trial-out leaks each participant's own bias into training and inflates the apparent correction; the paper's claim is a user-independent model, which only leave-one-participant-out supports"
    },
    {
      "question": "In what order are the correction percentages aggregated - per environment then per method, or pooled over all trials?",
      "where": "Modeling, p. 6, which prints one percentage per method and one overall",
      "assumed": "Mean of the two per-environment percentages, then the mean over the four methods",
      "sensitivity": "Low but decisive for reproduction: this reading recovers 29.3% exactly, whereas pooling the cell means first gives 29.5%"
    },
    {
      "question": "What reference frame defines alpha_pitch and alpha_yaw?",
      "where": "Modeling, p. 6: angles 'between the ray cast and the body'; 'the body' is never defined",
      "assumed": "World axes with +z the screen normal and the origin at the ray root",
      "sensitivity": "High. A shoulder- or head-anchored frame rotates the domain of the polynomial, so coefficients are not transferable between conventions - and since none were published, a reader cannot detect the mismatch"
    },
    {
      "question": "Which model family, fitted to which data, does Study 2 apply in real time?",
      "where": "Apparatus, p. 7: 'the Unity scene was adjusted to support our model if needed'",
      "assumed": "f4, per environment, for EFRC, fitted on all 20 Study-1 participants",
      "sensitivity": "Moderate; this is the assumption most favourable to the paper, since f4 on all data is the strongest form of the model"
    },
    {
      "question": "Was the correction used in Study 2 the cross-validated model or the all-data fit?",
      "where": "Not stated anywhere in Evaluation, pp. 7-8",
      "assumed": "The all-data fit",
      "sensitivity": "Moderate. Either is user-independent with respect to the 16 new participants, but the all-data fit is better trained, so a LOOCV-based deployment would likely have shown a smaller improvement than 13.1%"
    },
    {
      "question": "What instant of a Study-2 trial defines the recorded pointing endpoint?",
      "where": "Evaluation, p. 7 defines TCT by the button press but never ties the accuracy measure to an instant",
      "assumed": "The ray-screen intersection at the moment of the click",
      "sensitivity": "High in the with-cursor conditions, where participants actively null their error: averaging a hold window instead would report a smaller offset than an instantaneous sample"
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the cursor's size, update rate and smoothing?",
      "where": "Apparatus, p. 7: 'a green crosshair as suggested by Olsen and Nielsen', with a 30 FPS tracking system stated on p. 4",
      "assumed": "Not implemented; this repository renders nothing",
      "sensitivity": "High for the paper's own result: cursor gain, latency and smoothing directly determine the 1.1-1.3 cm with-cursor offsets and the TCT increase the abstract claims"
    },
