{
  "schema": 1,
  "verdict": "partial",
  "summary": "The inner loop of this CHI 2018 paper is a geometry-plus-polynomial pipeline (four ray casts from tracked body markers, offsets on a projection screen, a 15-parameter complete bivariate quartic fitted per angle, leave-one-out cross-validation) followed by two three-way RM-ANOVAs. All of it is re-implementable from the prose and is implemented and run here, but the authors released no pointing data and none of the ~240 fitted coefficients that are the paper's stated contribution, so no cell of Tables 1-3 can be recomputed from source. What can be checked without data was checked exhaustively: of 59 checks, all 35 derivations the paper's own tables can support hold (per-method mean offsets, the LOOCV correction percentages including the 29.3% headline, the Study 2 improvements of 13.1% and 4.5%, the cross-paper ratios against Mayer et al. 2015, and every design count), 4 more are not derivable from anything published, and 6 of the 20 reported F/p pairs fail recomputation - including a main effect the paper calls significant, ENVIRONMENT on accuracy, reported as F(1,15)=1.3, p=.027 where that F gives p=.272. The outer loop is two lab studies with 36 participants wearing an HTC Vive under an OptiTrack rig; it was not attempted, not simulated and not scored, and the paper's human findings are neither confirmed nor disputed here.",
  "graded_at": "2026-08-16T10:05:00Z",
  "components": { "verified": 8, "partial": 4, "blocked": 3, "total": 15 },
  "boundary": [
    {
      "claim": "Four ray casts (EFRC, IFRC, FRC, HRC) constructed from tracked markers and intersected with the projection screen",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "partial",
      "evidence": "Implemented in src/geometry.py; exact on the 7x5 grid and on the angle round-trip (python src/selftest.py). Cannot be checked against the paper: the marker-to-anatomy transforms (cyclops eye from HMD markers, 'true finger tip' from marker plus per-user measurement, forearm centre 'approximating the forearm with a frustum of a cone') are prose only, and no marker data was released.",
      "citation": "Apparatus, p. 4"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Preprocessing: 100-900 ms sample window, offset defined as ray-screen intersection to target, two-standard-deviation outlier bound, head alignment across participants",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "partial",
      "evidence": "Implemented in src/preprocess.py and run end to end (./run_all.sh; 32,111 synthetic trials survive filtering). Three of the four steps are under-specified (decisions D1-D4) and the paper never reports how many trials the filter removed, so no count can be matched.",
      "citation": "Preprocessing, p. 5"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Average offset per ray cast over both environments: 9.33 cm EFRC, 28.09 cm IFRC, 65.0 cm FRC, 42.46 cm HRC",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "Recomputed from Table 1 to within 0.005 cm for all four methods (python src/check_paper.py, ids s1-mean-*).",
      "citation": "Accuracy of Ray Casts, p. 5, from Table 1"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The correction model family f1-f4, with f4 the 15-parameter function of Equation 1, fitted by a nonlinear least-squares solver",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "The 15 terms of Equation 1 are exactly the complete bivariate quartic; the basis is reconstructed term by term and asserted in src/selftest.py. The model is linear in x0..x14, and the paper's nonlinear solver agrees with a linear least-squares solve to 6.5e-10, so the stated solver choice is immaterial.",
      "citation": "Modeling, p. 6, Equation 1"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The fitted correction models themselves: 4 ray casts x 2 environments x 2 angles x 15 coefficients",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "blocked",
      "evidence": "Blocker: never published, in the paper or at any of the 22 locations searched (SOURCES.md). The paper's headline contribution therefore cannot be applied by a reader even in principle. The predecessor paper did print its coefficients (Mayer et al. 2015, Table 3); they are re-implemented here in src/mayer2015_model.py to show what the missing artifact would look like.",
      "citation": "Modeling, p. 6"
    },
    {
      "claim": "LOOCV correction achieved: FRC 55.9%, IFRC 50.1%, EFRC 10.9%, HRC 0.2%, overall 29.3%; remaining offsets 8.2, 14.0, 28.6, 42.3 cm",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "All nine figures reproduce from Table 1 (check ids s1-correction-*, s1-remaining-*, s1-correction-overall): the per-method value is the mean of the two per-environment percentages and the overall value is the mean of those four. The aggregation order and the CV granularity are unstated (D5, D6).",
      "citation": "Modeling, p. 6, from Table 1",
      "mismatch": {
        "kind": "typo",
        "reported": "EFRC correction 10.9%, HRC remaining offset 42.3 cm",
        "observed": "10.96% and 42.385 cm; the paper truncates rather than rounds, here and in about six other derived figures",
        "delta": "<= 0.09 in the last printed digit"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Four one-way RM-ANOVAs comparing RealWorld with VirtualReality per ray cast (EFRC F(1,19)=5.845 p=.026; IFRC F=.447 p=.512; FRC F=33.13 p<.001; HRC F=31.48 p<.001)",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "blocked",
      "evidence": "Blocker: the 8,400 postures were never released, so no F can be recomputed. All four reported F/p pairs are internally consistent, which is the most that can be established from the text.",
      "citation": "Accuracy of Ray Casts, p. 5"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Fatigue checks: raw-TLX means per session and a one-way RM-ANOVA in each study, both non-significant",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "partial",
      "evidence": "Study 1's F(3,57)=.047, p=.986 is internally consistent and its df match 20 participants over 4 sessions. Study 2's is not, on two counts (see mismatch). The conclusion drawn (no fatigue effect) is a null result that neither error overturns.",
      "citation": "Results, p. 5 and Results, p. 8",
      "mismatch": {
        "kind": "contradiction",
        "reported": "F(5,15) = .654, p = .659 for six raw-TLX sessions with 16 participants",
        "observed": "the design implies error df = 75, not 15 (Study 1 uses the analogous F(3,57) correctly); and F(5,15) = .654 gives p = .663",
        "delta": "df2 -60; p +.004"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Study 2 condition tables - TCT (Table 2) and remaining offset (Table 3) - produced under a design that collapses 8 factorial cells into 6 administered conditions",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "The design's fingerprint is visible in the paper's own numbers: with no cursor a participant cannot perceive whether correction is applied, and Table 2's no-cursor TCT is identical across CORRECTION in both environments (1.48/1.48 and 1.64/1.64), exactly as the collapse requires (check ids s2-tct-nocursor-identical-*). Table 3's no-cursor offsets do differ, as they must, since correction still moves the computed intersection.",
      "citation": "Study Design, p. 7; Tables 2 and 3"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Correction improvements: 13.1% overall without a cursor, 16.3% RealWorld, 9.5% VirtualWorld, 4.5% with a cursor",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "All four reproduce from Table 3 (check ids s2-improve-*). The two aggregate figures come out at 13.1% and 4.5% only if the cell means are pooled before the ratio is taken; averaging the two per-environment percentages instead gives 12.98% and 4.31% (decision D14).",
      "citation": "Discussion p. 9 and Conclusion, from Table 3"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Two three-way RM-ANOVAs over CORRECTION x CURSOR x ENVIRONMENT, on accuracy and on task completion time",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "partial",
      "evidence": "The analysis is re-implemented (src/pipeline.py:study2 using statsmodels AnovaRM) and runs on study-2-shaped data, reproducing the F(1,15) structure the paper's df imply. The paper's values cannot be recomputed (no trials released), and 5 of the 14 reported F/p pairs fail recomputation.",
      "citation": "Results, p. 8",
      "mismatch": {
        "kind": "contradiction",
        "reported": "'a significant effect of ... ENVIRONMENT, F(1,15) = 1.3, p = .027 on the participants' pointing accuracy', restated as a finding in the Discussion",
        "observed": "F(1,15) = 1.3 gives p = .272; on the printed F the effect is not significant. Four further pairs also fail: accuracy CORRECTION p = .027 vs .036; accuracy CORRECTION x ENVIRONMENT p = .36 vs .337; TCT ENVIRONMENT p = .956 vs .950; TCT CURSOR x ENVIRONMENT reported p < .001 vs .00128",
        "delta": "p +.245 on ENVIRONMENT; flips the significance decision at alpha = .05"
      }
    },
    {
      "claim": "Square target sizes holding 90% of pointing actions: 17.6 cm (no cursor, RW), 18.8 cm (no cursor, VR), 4.1 cm and 4.5 cm with cursor, and the per-condition shrinks of 6.9, 11.6, 6.5 and 8.9%",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "blocked",
      "evidence": "Blocker: needs the per-trial two-dimensional endpoints; Table 3 publishes only the mean and SD of the scalar distance, from which a square capture region is not recoverable. The estimator is implemented and validated against its analytic value (src/pipeline.py:target_size_90; selftest gives 11.69 cm vs 11.69 cm). Forcing an isotropic Rayleigh assumption onto Table 3 - my assumption, not the paper's - would give 22.0 cm where the paper reports 17.6 cm.",
      "citation": "Accuracy, p. 8"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Targets can on average be 8.5% smaller when the correction models are applied",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "Mean of the paper's own four per-condition shrinks (6.9, 11.6, 6.5, 8.9) = 8.475% (check id s2-target-shrink-mean).",
      "citation": "Future Work, p. 9"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Comparison with the predecessor study: errors before correction 4.8x (EFRC), 1.9x (IFRC) and 3.7x (FRC) larger in Mayer et al. 2015; IFRC 89.2% larger than EFRC after correction here; EFRC 4.9% larger than IFRC there",
      "loop": "inner",
      "outcome": "verified",
      "evidence": "All five numbers reproduce against Mayer et al. 2015 Tables 1-2, row 'standing 2m', and against Table 1 here (check ids s1-ratio-2015-*, s1-ifrc-vs-efrc, s1-2015-efrc-vs-ifrc).",
      "citation": "Model Discussion, p. 6",
      "mismatch": {
        "kind": "numeric",
        "reported": "'Overall Mayer et al. reported errors before correction 4.8 times larger for EFRC, 1.9 for IFRC and 3.7 for FRC'",
        "observed": "4.76, 1.90, 3.69 if 'overall' means the RealWorld column alone; 5.21, 1.97, 3.43 if it means the average over both environments, which is how 'overall' is used elsewhere in the paper",
        "delta": "+0.41 on EFRC under the other reading"
      }
